And I assume that you will be happy to let the judges decide, after
viewing my comprehensive disproof of your design, on whether they need
to run a simulation?  To agree on suitable terms for a simulation we
must first define the experiment precisely.  To agree on suitable terms
without running a simulation we must define the experiment more.  Either
way we must define the experiment.  To that end answer the questions I
asked earlier.  Rephrasing my questions:
-    Are we assuming a noise-free system?  (My answer = Yes)
-    Is a*C*norm >> N0+N1+N2 .... +Nm in the final hamiltonian?  
     In other words, that "a" is arbitrarily large or that the 
     N-terms are to be ignored in the limit as time goes to infinity?
     (My answer = Yes)
-    that we are ignoring any bouncing (transitions induced by the
     changing hamiltonian) from one energy level of the
     instantaneous hamiltonian to another?  (My answer = Yes)

So far you have failed to answer any of the points.  Your long
explanation about noise singularly failed to answer the question posed. 
You argued that "we can legitimately ignore noise."  This does _not_
answer what I asked.  I asked, would we be _assuming_ there is no noise
to simplify matters.  Pardon my pedantry.  Your inability or refusal to
parse quite simple statements continues to disappoint me.  Your
continual obfuscation or evasion convinces me of the need to have the
terms and conditions of this wager nailed down precisely.  Remember this
is a gedankenexperiment - we can eliminate noise by fiat.

> Remember that if I fail to write the simulation, or Seth
> Lloyd judges that the simulation fails to simulate the
> quantum computer it purports to, or the simulation fails to
> show the behavior I predict [...]

This is not good enough.  Seth Lloyd, or whoever, has to make too many
judgements in areas where he may have no expertise.  First he has to
check all your theory.  Then check your simulator code against the
theory (is he a programmer?).  Then run the simulation (is he PC-
fluent).  Then collate the data (is he a statistican?).  Then make a
value judgement on the closeness of fit between prediction and result. 
My claim is simpler.  Your theory is screwed.  And at an elementary
level.  At one precise point - where you claim that the system remains
in the ground state.  Let the judge(s) settle the issue at that stage
about this one precise area.  Either way, you still have to define how
you expect the system to behave, so please answer my earlier questions.

