From: kqb@whscad1.att.com
Subject: Targetted Bio Weapons & True Names

How feasible is it to target a (viral) disease to an individual person
within the next 10 years?  Except for identical twins, everyone has unique
DNA and, presumably, everyone has a unique immune system.  That should
give the designers something unique to target.  Also, bear in mind that
the disease must not only be finely targetted, but also highly resistant
to mutation!  If it self-destructs after a short period, all the better.

Assuming that such individually targetted diseases can be designed and
produced soon, can they be delivered in such a way that nobody can trace
their origin?  If so, what are the consequences for highly public
government officials?  Or any famous people?  Or anyone who irritates
one too many people?  Will this favor small-scale terrorist attacks more
than large-scale war machine attacks? **  Will this make revealing your
True Name a deadly mistake?  Or will it become just another minor risk
in life, such as crossing the street without getting run over?

Of course, if nobody can get samples of your DNA, you are safe, right?
But how feasible is that?  How many ways do we shed samples of our DNA?
Little flecks of skin fly away wherever we walk, don't they?  Shaking
hands with someone gives an obvious opportunity to scrape off a few
skin cells.  Blood donations won't give you AIDS but they certainly
will give away your DNA.  Toilets may become treasure troves of
valuable genetic information.

Advanced Question: What is the average Kolmogorov complexity of a
virus targetted to an individual human?  :-)  Would the extra coding
to achieve this fine-targetting increase the size of the virus so much
that it could not compete with the other micro-organisms already in
the environment?  If so, then perhaps only some, unluckily unique,
individuals can be targetted by a viable virus?

Disclaimer: I am not interested in developing or applying any such
technology.  I'm just writing a little story and would like to know
if this idea is too silly to consider.  Also, in case you wondered,
yes, this year's cold & flu season has arrived...

