Date: Mon, 01 Nov 1993 19:35:58
From: tim.hruby@his.com (Tim Hruby)
Subject: META: A Proposal for Easy Ratings of Articles and Authors

I too agree that a simple ratings scheme would be a fun thing to play
with.

However, it is important to realize that, like HEx, it will not really
serve its intended purpose....  Well, I'm not really sure what the intended
purpose is.  For the reasons Tim May mentions (mainly trading for profit, 
and not for the "common good"), HEx doesn't reflect the value of people's
posts to the list.  Rather, HEx values truly represent a pure form of
"reputation":  what people are willing to pay for something that has no
intrinsic value, in a currency that has little intrinsic value.  I say
"little intrinsic vlaue" because thornes are valuable for one thing: you
can get Nick Szabo to retreive information for you.  HEx values have almost
no use in controlling the list S/N ratio, tho I suppose one could ::exclude
all posters whose net worth was below a certain value or somesuch (given
current market valuations, IMO this would be a horrid strategy for trying
to improve the S/N one got out of the list).  Don't get me wrong, HEx is
lots of fun to play around with, and I've even thought of offering to
broker PGP signed futures contracts, loan out my holdings for people
who wish to sell short, and loan out thornes to investors (i.e., be the
first stockbroker, and first _operating_ bank on HEx.)  Never got around
to it tho....

But back to this ratings system.  There are still many problems with it.

[1]  As Tim May mentioned many moons ago when such things were discussed
before, who is going to trust those people who rank posts to censor out
what you will and won't get from the List?  Not me;  I'll decide on my
own ::excludes and ::includes, based on who and what I think is worth
listening to.  Still, perhaps the rankings would be useful, in that they
provide feedback to people who post inane or excellent items.  That gets
me to my next point, however....

[2]  Similar to the problem of HEx, one has to ask just what do these 
ratings mean?  They are the average (or maybe some kind of weighted average)
of the subjective values of those people who ranked the posts, and thus,
their virtue is supposed to be that they represent some kind of "market
value" of the posts.  However, there is a major market failure here.
Just who is going to bother to rank postings, and what keeps them honest?

The major problem here is that there is no incentive for anyone to go 
to the extra trouble of rating posts, an activity whose benefit inures
solely to precisely those people who do *not* rank posts, but instead 
rely on the rankings to filter out the noise.  Rather, those people who 
send in rankings will be motivated by concerns of personal utility:  i.e.,
novelty, fun, revenge & sabotage for other slights, etc.  These people 
will not be a representative sample of List members; it will be those of 
us hacking the system (which I'll probably be guilty of).  Then, most 
likely, after much of the novelty wears off, activity will become very 
sparse.  Even if we manage to add an incentive for taking the time to 
rate posts (such as by providing List credits, if and when we wind up
going to a pay-for-the-list scheme) there still is no mechanism for
keeping people "honest", by which I mean rating for information content
rather than for other reasons.  If I like Perry, and don't like Tim May,
why don't I just rate all of Perry's posts "10" and all of Tim's "0"?
There's no cost to my lying; I still get the same reward in terms of 
list credits or thornes or whatever.  For the system to "work", you have 
to reward people for sending in ratings, and for sending in honest ones.
That may almost be an insurmountable difficulty.

I see a possible solution here in Robin Hanson's Idea Futures.  If you
devalue my reward based on how far off the norm I am, and increase it
by how close to the norm I come, then you are encouraging everybody to
guess what the average value will be.  Thus, they are rating not their
own personal view of the information, but what they think it is worth
to everybody else.  If they are right, they win big; if not, the reward
is smaller, or maybe non-existent, or even a penalty.  I haven't completely
thought through this idea, and I'd appreciate hearing Robin or anyone
else's ideas on it.

Still, though, my first question remains.  Even if we get an accurate
"market value" of the information content of a post, what do we do with
this information?  Unless you are involved in arbitrage, you don't buy
something simply because the market values it highly; you buy it because
you value it more than the market does.  Just because the market highly
values the thread on "Why Space Aliens will never allow Perry's brain
to grow larger than Mars" doesn't mean that I care about it at all.  
Likewise, just because the market doesn't value the thread on "How 
skipping stones on a lake can add 30 years to your life-expectancy"
(because most people on the list don't live near a lake) doesn't mean 
that I (with my own private lake) don't want to hear the news.

Not to put a damper on the parade for this list add-on, but I think
before a considerable investment of programming resources goes into it,
we should determine what beneifts it provides, besides the temporary
spurt of fun we'll all have playing with it.



Tim

